1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. c3 d6 4. d4 Nf6 5. Bb5 Be7 6. Bg5 O-O 7. Bxc6 bxc6 8.
Qd3 Nh5 9. h4 f5 10. Nbd2 d5 11. exd5 e4 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Qe3 cxd5 14. g3 f4
15. gxf4 Nxf4 16. Rg1 Ba6 17. O-O-O Ne2+ 18. Kb1 Bd3+ 19. Ka1 Rab8 20. Rge1
Rxb2 21. Kxb2 Nxc3 22. Kxc3 Qa3+ 23. Nb3 Rxf3 24. Qxf3 exf3 25. Rxd3 Qxa2 26.
Rxf3 Qa4 27. Kb2 h6 28. Ra1 Qc4 29. Rc3 {Sources: Staffordshire Advertiser, 16
May 1925; BCM, May 1925, p216. The score of the game in BCM (from which the
newspaper score may have been taken) gives the players as Bruning (White) and
Pratten (Black) but the detailed description of the game on p214 makes it
fairly certain that Pratten played White in the game: "In the last round
Pratten very nearly had a shock. Opening with a Ponziani, which turned into a
kind of Ruy Lopez after Bruning had played 3.., P-Q 3, he got a strong-looking
game; but Bruning defended so well that the situation changed entirely to the
holder�s disadvantage, and at last a sacrifice was obviously Black�s
policy. Bruning saw this, but unfortunately sacrificed the wrong piece, and
Pratten, by giving up his Queen, for adequate compensation, was able to
extricate himself, and a draw resulted." But we cannot be certain. JS} 1/2-1/2

