1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. exd6 exd6 6. Bd3 Be7 7. Ne2 Bg4 8.
O-O Nc6 9. f3 Bh5 10. Ng3 Bg6 11. Nf5 Bxf5 12. Bxf5 g6 13. Bh3 O-O 14. Qd3 Bf6
15. Be3 Re8 16. Bf2 Bg7 17. Na3 a6 18. Nc2 Nd7 19. b4 a5 20. b5 Nb4 21. Nxb4
axb4 22. Qd2 Nb6 23. Rac1 d5 24. c5 Nc4 25. Qxb4 Rxa2 26. Rfe1 Ree2 27. Rxe2
Rxe2 28. g3 Qe8 29. Rd1 Rxf2 30. Kxf2 Qe3+ 31. Kf1 Qxf3+ 32. Ke1 Qe3+ 33. Kf1
Bxd4 34. Rxd4 Qxd4 35. Qe1 Nd2+ 36. Kg2 Qe4+ {'and Black soon won'. In 1991
when John Cannon was writing an article on Jack Smith, the latter produced a
cutting from a Portsmouth newspaper (probably the Evening News), which gave
the score of the above game. Subsequently this game was published in 'Sussex
Chess 1991'. There does not seem to have been a chess column in the Portsmouth
newspaper, the Evening News, in 1946 and it is possible that the game was
published some years later. Enquiries of Roger Leslie Paige, who has in his
possession several games of Wilfred Pratten, did not reveal the existence of
any more of the moves in the game. Source (and comments): Brian Denman. Added
to BritBase, 4 June 2019} 0-1

