1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c5 4. Nf3 d5 5. cxd5 exd5 6. g3 Nc6 7. Bg2 Be7 8. O-O
O-O 9. dxc5 Be6 10. a3 Bxc5 11. b4 Be7 12. Bf4 Ne4 13. Rc1 Rc8 14. Nb5 a5 15.
bxa5 Qxa5 16. a4 Bf6 17. Nd2 Be5 18. Nxe4 Bxf4 19. gxf4 dxe4 20. Bxe4 Qb4 21.
Qd3 Rfd8 22. Bxh7+ Kh8 23. Qe4 g6 24. Qxb4 Nxb4 25. Rxc8 Rxc8 26. f5 Bxf5 27.
Nd6 Rc5 28. Nxf5 Rxf5 29. Rb1 Rg5+ 30. Kf1 Rg4 31. Bxg6 fxg6 32. f3 Rc4 33. Rd1
Rc7 34. Rd6 Kg7 35. Kf2 Kf7 36. f4 Re7 37. Kf3 Na6 38. e3 Nc5 39. a5 Nb3 40. a6
bxa6 41. Rxa6 Nd2+ 42. Kg4 Rxe3 43. Ra7+ Kf6 44. Ra6+ Kg7 45. Ra2 Rd3 46. Ra4
Nf1 47. Ra2 Ne3+ 48. Kg5 Rd5+ 49. Kh4 Rh5+ 50. Kg3 Nf5+ 51. Kf3 Rh8 52. Ra7+
Kh6 53. Rd7 Rc8 54. Rd2 Kh5 55. Ra2 Rc3+ 56. Ke4 Re3+ 57. Kd5 Kg4 58. Rf2 Rf3
59. Rg2+ Kxf4 60. Ke6 Nh4 {Source: WH Pratten book by RL Paige (2004), which
gives it as Taylor 0-1 Pratten. However, I'm convinced this is wrong and that
it was Taylor who won with Black. The evidence: the Times, 13 Sept 1961, gives
the result of the adjourned game as "Taylor beat Pratten". And the report of
the tournament in BCM (Nov 1961, p306) says that Alexander led from start to
finish but that "the Oxford University player G. C. Taylor managed to stay
with him for four rounds." This makes it extremely unlikely that Taylor should
have lost his first game. A third and perhaps clinching piece of evidence is
that the Paige book has three other games which Pratten drew at Paignton that
year, and his final score was 1½/7, therefore he cannot have won this game
too - JS} 0-1

